;;; hash-table-ref-value-default.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · broken-examples
;;;
;;; A broken-example is kept ON PURPOSE (CLAUDE.md § Broken-examples teach
;;; the differences). This one is the most dangerous shape in the whole hash
;;; library: it RUNS AND RETURNS THE RIGHT ANSWER when the key is present, and
;;; only RAISES when the key is missing — which is precisely the moment a
;;; default was supposed to help. A test that seeds the key before reading it
;;; passes; production, where the key is sometimes absent, throws.
;;;
;;; Provenance for the DECISION: Motoi conformed `hash-table-ref` to strict
;;; SRFI-125 on 2026-07-24. Under SRFI-125 the third argument is a FAILURE
;;; THUNK (called with no args on a miss), not a plain default value. The old
;;; corpus was written against a value-default reading. The behavior-preserving
;;; fix is to spell a constant fallback with `hash-table-ref/default`.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "broken-example — hash-table-ref third arg is a thunk, not a value")

(broken-example :name "hash-table-ref"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-ref-failure-thunk-contract"
  :std-target "SRFI-125"
  :std-rejected ("SRFI-69-value-default-reading")
  :status "conformed"
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme/reference/broken-examples/hash-table-ref-value-default.slatl"

  ;; ─── THE BROKEN CODE (runs on a hit, raises on a miss) ───
  :broken-code "(define h (make-hash-table))\n(display (hash-table-ref h 'missing 'my-default))\n(newline)"
  :broken-output "error: not a function: my-default"
  :runs "no on a miss — but YES on a hit, which is the trap"

  ;; ─── WHY IT IS BROKEN ───
  :why
"SRFI-125 specifies `(hash-table-ref h k [failure [success]])` where `failure`
is a THUNK of no arguments, invoked only when k is absent, whose return value
becomes the result. Motoi's engine follows the spec literally: on a miss it
tries to CALL the third argument. A plain value like the symbol `my-default`
is not callable, so the runtime raises `not a function: my-default`.

The trap is the hit case. When the key IS present the third argument is never
touched, so

    (hash-table-ref h 'k 'my-default)   ; k present  => the stored value

returns correctly and the bug stays invisible. Seed-then-read unit tests pass;
the failure only surfaces the first time a real miss occurs in production."

  ;; ─── THE FIX ───
  :fix-code "(define h (make-hash-table))\n(display (hash-table-ref/default h 'missing 'my-default))\n(newline)"
  :fix-output "my-default"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note
"When the fallback is a constant, use `hash-table-ref/default` — its third
argument IS a plain value. When the fallback needs to be computed lazily or
should raise, keep `hash-table-ref` and pass an actual thunk:
`(hash-table-ref h 'k (lambda () (compute-default)))`. Omitting the thunk
entirely is also legal and raises `key not found` — the strict form."

  ;; ─── THE LESSON ───
  :lesson
"Two verbs, one letter of difference in intent: `hash-table-ref` is strict and
its optional third arg is a THUNK; `hash-table-ref/default` is lenient and its
third arg is a VALUE. Never pass a bare value to `hash-table-ref` — it will be
called. The danger is that the wrong form type-checks nowhere and passes every
test that pre-seeds the key. Reach for `/default` whenever the fallback is a
constant."

  ;; ─── THE SAME IDEA IN THREE OTHER LIBRARIES ───
  :three-other-libs (
    (:lib "SRFI-125"
     :status "hash-table-ref failure arg is a thunk; hash-table-ref/default takes a value"
     :code "(hash-table-ref/default h 'k 'fallback)   ; => fallback"
     :note "The target contract. The /default variant exists exactly so callers do not wrap constants in lambdas.")
    (:lib "SRFI-69"
     :status "hash-table-ref/default is identical; hash-table-ref also took a thunk"
     :code "(hash-table-ref/default h 'k 'fallback)"
     :note "SRFI-125 is largely SRFI-69-compatible here; the /default spelling is stable across both.")
    (:lib "Racket"
     :status "hash-ref failure arg may be a value OR a thunk (procedure? is dispatched on)"
     :code "(hash-ref h 'k 'fallback)   ; value used directly"
     :note "Racket overloads on whether the failure arg is a procedure. Motoi/SRFI-125 does NOT — it always calls it — which is why the Racket habit ports as a bug.")
  )

  :motoi-standard
"Target: strict SRFI-125 `hash-table-ref` (third arg = failure thunk, absent =
raise) PLUS `hash-table-ref/default` (third arg = value). Conformed in the
engine 2026-07-24. Corpus constant-default sites migrated to `/default`. This
counter-example is kept to teach the thunk-vs-value distinction and the
hit-passes/miss-raises trap."

  ;; ─── THE KIND THIS IS AN INSTANCE OF (up-pointer to the error census) ───
  :generalized-by ("../error-cards/silent-non-errors.slatl"
                   "../error-cards/not-a-procedure.slatl"))
